A small, secluded tropical island roughly 2 miles in diameter. From the ocean it resembles a massive ragged tree stump rising out of the water. It features lush jungle, dramatic cliffs, and a large bay on the north side with its opening facing west. The island has one extinct volcanic mountain on the western side.

A large, beautiful bay on the north side of the island. The bay is mostly surrounded by sandy beaches. North Beach curves into East Beach, which connects to South Beach. The water is calm and turquoise inside the bay.

On the southwestern side of the bay, steep cliffs rise from the water. A wooden dock is built along the base of the cliffs. From the dock, a long set of old wooden switchback stairs climbs approximately 30 feet up the cliff face. Halfway up there is a scenic landing with a small deck and a couple of chairs, offering a beautiful view of the bay. Often used for diving into the bay.

A modern open-concept house located south of the South Beach. It has large windows with excellent views of the bay and sits a short distance inland.

An extinct volcanic mountain located southwest of the house. A scenic waterfall flows down the eastern side of the mountain into a small pond. A stream then flows from the pond eastward, crossing a small fantasy-style wooden bridge.

A beautiful overlook point near the top of the cliffs or on a high point of the mountain trail. It offers panoramic views of the bay and ocean. There are a couple of benches here for watching sunsets.

In a sunny clearing near the house sits a massive, smooth-cut tree stump roughly 15 feet in diameter and cut about 3 feet off the ground. It functions as a natural platform. A few comfortable chairs are usually placed around it. This spot gets plenty of sunlight and has become a favorite private place for relaxation and intimate moments.

South Beach underfoot is fine white sand, soft and hot in the sun, sloping gently into calm turquoise water that laps quietly at the shore. Coconut palms lean over the sand, dropping shade in moving patches, and the air smells of salt, sun-warmed vegetation, and coconut oil. The surf is a low hiss, not a crash, inside the bay.

Days run bright and hot with a steady trade breeze moving through the palms; nights bring loud insects and a black sky full of stars. Sunsets drop through the bay's west-facing opening and turn the whole anchorage gold and pink. Rain comes fast and leaves faster.

A standing beach setup waits near the house path on South Beach: loungers, a shade awning, and a cooler kept stocked with drinks and ice. Towels live in a deck box by the awning poles.
